WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.910
- Res Ipsa Loquitur.

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:03.210
Abra cadabra.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.160
Caveat emptor.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.540
Avarda cadavra.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.300
Why do lawyers use such funny words?

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.190
It actually it has
nothing to do with lawyers

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.840
inventing highfalutin legal
words in order to sound smart.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.010
We're that already.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.430
And as a graduate of both
law school and Hogwarts,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.370
I can tell you that lawyers
don't have magic words.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.600
But over time, all professions build up

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.830
a special vocabulary
to help describe things

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.490
in very specific language.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.100
And usually, it's not magic.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.190
But sometimes it is sort of magic.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.960
And when Mark Jaffe posted on Twitter,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.130
What genuine law term sounds fake?

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.390
You have to convince people
you didn't make it up?

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.800
I knew I had to share some of the most

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.610
ridiculous legal terms out there.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.070
And if I have to deal with
these legal terms every day,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.686
I'm taking you Legal Eagles with me.

00:00:45.686 --> 00:00:47.990
(bright music)

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.200
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.230
All right, so the first
ridiculous legal term

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.970
that we have to deal
with is Pendente lite.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.990
Now you might think this
is a Mexican low-cal beer.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.060
Pendente lite, taste the lawyer.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.260
Or maybe, it's a fancy pendant light,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.800
or perhaps, it is the lowest
weight class in boxing,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.500
pendente lite, one step below
the 108-pound flyweight.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.780
But what it actually is,
has nothing to do with

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.490
YouTubers turning into boxers.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.830
Pendente lite is a Latin term

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.720
meaning awaiting the litigation
or pending the litigation.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.010
You often see this during
divorce and custody litigation.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.360
A court can issue a
temporary order of support,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.300
pendente lite, to maintain
the financial status quo

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.800
of all parties of the
divorce while it's pending.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.270
And court orders are often
used to provide relief

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.130
until the final judgment is rendered.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.700
And the adverbial form of this,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.810
it would be a lis pendens,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.480
but that's also its own
special legal document,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.000
usually regarding real estate.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.560
Oh God, this is already
getting incredibly complicated,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.330
but onto the second ridiculous
term, which is champerty.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.720
Now you might think that champerty

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.220
is a character from
the TV show, Bridgerton

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.610
announcing Lady Champerty of
the North Hampstead Sherford

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.420
upon Tim's Champertys.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.690
Might be a fraternity drinking game.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:56.960
It might be something people

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.020
on HGTV's House Hunters would complain

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.800
about not having in their home,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.630
like the champerty water
fountain for their dogs.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.710
But what it actually is is when

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.770
a third party funds litigation.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:08.180
Now, historically, this was known

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.850
as maintenance and champerty.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:10.683
Back in the middle ages,

00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:13.080
nobles started funding
frivolous lawsuits to buttress

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.560
the claims and take a share
of any profits for themselves.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.610
And Black's law dictionary defines this as

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.957
a champertous agreement, as a quote,

00:02:19.957 --> 00:02:21.200
"bargain between a stranger

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.700
and a party to a lawsuit by
which the stranger pursues

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.200
the parties claims in consideration

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.670
for receiving part of any
judgment of the proceeds."

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.780
And perhaps the most interesting
thing is that these days,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.530
these kinds of agreements
are generally not illegal.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.160
There's a whole world of
litigation finance out there,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.220
that in the olden days,

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.350
would have been considered
champerty and illegal.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.100
For example, now a hedge fund
could invest in litigation

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.250
that the plaintiffs couldn't
afford to fund on their own.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:47.160
It actually allows more people
to access the justice system

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.090
than otherwise would be able
to do because of the funds

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.560
that they or their lawyers
do or don't possess.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.630
But on the other hand,

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.910
the funding party could be a
billionaire who has a grudge

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:57.743
against someone else.

00:02:57.743 --> 00:03:00.370
As we saw with the Peter Thiel lawsuits

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.570
through Hulk Hogan against Gawker.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:04.330
So you know, a double-edged sword.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.900
But that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.300
which is Mutatis Mutandis,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.240
which could be the High Valyrian
for all lawyers must die.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.410
Or it could be an outtake

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.550
from the Lion King giving Timon and Pumbaa

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.280
a followup to Hakuna Matata.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.360
- What a wonderful phrase.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.620
- Or it could be the title given

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.440
to the most important
praying mantis in the world.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.930
Well, actually it's a Latin phrase

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.680
that means with things
change that should be changed

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.980
or having changed what
needs to be changed.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.600
It pops up a lot in legal circles

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.470
when lawyers are drafting contracts.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.350
The simplest explanation
is for use in the law

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.680
and that the necessary changes in details

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.390
such as names and places will be made

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.300
but everything else will remain the same.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.120
That explanation comes from
the famous Judge Scheindlin.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.440
No, not that Judge Scheindlin,
Judge Shira Scheindlin

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:49.710
who is well-known to all lawyers

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.820
for her round breaking decisions
in electronic discovery

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.720
which still formed the
backbone of federal rules

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.490
that govern electronic
information in litigation

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.970
which takes us to the next
ridiculous legal term.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.990
The suggestion of death.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.410
Now is the suggestion
of death a proper way

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.930
of insulting opposing counsel?

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.800
I moved to strike opposing
counsel upon suggestion of death.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.030
Is it a spell or event
from Dungeons and Dragons?

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.280
Is it what happens in the Oregon Trail

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.240
when you unsuccessfully
tried to forward a river?

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.760
Or is it a Charles Bronson
movie suggestion of death

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.580
from the producers of
Death Wish and Chasing Amy?

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.730
Well, actually, according
to Henry Campbell Black

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.380
the man who literally wrote
the book On Legal Terms,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.350
he explains that the suggestion of death

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.300
is a quote, "statement
formally entered on the record

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.090
of some fact or circumstance
which will materially affect

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.920
further proceedings in the cause."

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.160
I know it looks weird since
a person's death is usually

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.230
not a suggestion, it's a fact,

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.810
but a suggestion of death functions

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:48.360
as a legal announcement of death
and FYI to inform the court

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.590
and opposing parties that
a key person has died.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.040
A case doesn't necessarily go away

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.670
just because someone dies
although that can happen.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.770
But this is just a lawyer's
way of letting everyone know

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.670
that something kind of major
has happened in the case.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.310
Which takes us to the next
ridiculous legal term,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:03.750
an attractive nuisance.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.930
Isn't an attractive nuisance

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.220
the person you run into
a bar at last call?

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.110
- You feeling what I'm feeling?

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.779
- If it's resignation then yes.

00:05:11.779 --> 00:05:12.800
(audience laughing)

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.110
- Is it the song that you
can't get out of your head?

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:16.940
Is it free cake in the office break room

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:17.830
when you're on a diet?

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.640
Is it when your pets or
children are annoying you

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.960
while you're trying to work from home

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.283
especially when you're on a zoom call?

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.273
- Pardon me.

00:05:26.273 --> 00:05:28.640
- Well, actually an attractive
nuisance is something

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.580
that's on your property that is attractive

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:32.780
and exciting to children.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:35.290
Property owners have a special
duty of care to children

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.320
and teens because they
can't fully understand

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.730
the dangers posed by certain situations.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.740
This means that if you
have a bucket of candy

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:44.680
or a PS5 sitting on a float
in your swimming pool,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.310
you might expect the children
are going to trespass

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:48.350
onto your property.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.100
And while often you're not
obligated to protect trespassers,

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.910
sometimes you have to
look out for those stupid,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.600
attractive, nuisance children.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.570
In other words, you're
obligated to mitigate the danger

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.190
by taking steps to prevent them
from accessing the property.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.880
Put up a fence, cover the pool

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.470
and keep your video games
downstairs in your mom's basement.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.980
Which takes us to the next
ridiculous legal term.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.530
A heartbalm tort.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.000
Now a heartbalm tort
could be an assignment

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.410
on The Great British Baking Show.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:15.950
Probably something really dumb,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.110
like a whole peach wrapped
in 1000 layers of pastry

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.710
and then baked for some
ridiculous amount of time

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.550
like 12 hours, could be, blink.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:26.680
- Love the flavor.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.710
It's a charming cake,
little bit of a mess.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.030
- Thank you so much.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:32.710
- It could be a magic spell.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.910
I command a level five
heart balm tort against you

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.570
or it could be a hallmark
movie about a down

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.160
and out heart surgeon Michael Bomb

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.840
who leaves his big city practice

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.690
to start up a bakery and falls in love

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:46.140
with a woman who can't
stop eating his mini pies.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.710
But actually a heartbalm tort

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.420
is a specific kind of
civil suit often referred

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:53.210
to as a tort that is
brought against someone

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.440
who has interfered in a
relationship or made a promise

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.273
that they didn't keep.

00:06:56.273 --> 00:06:58.250
This lets people sue
their spouses para moral

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.300
or bring charges against men
who promised to marry someone

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:02.690
but then backed out.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:05.250
These suits flourished
in the early 20th century

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.330
when newspapers featured lurid
tales of gold digging harlots

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:11.290
who broke up marriages and
then preyed on widowers.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.000
Eventually there were so
many heartbalm tort cases

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.830
that they were actually
abolished in more than 40 States.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.270
But that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term,

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:20.967
a frolic and detour.

00:07:20.967 --> 00:07:22.720
Now is a frolic and detour

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.210
a pair of animated mischievous kittens,

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.170
as in Tom and Jerry, Itchy and Scratchy?

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:29.030
Is it the new Michael Chabon's novel

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.270
or is it the newest Kardashian baby name?

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.830
Frolic and detour along
with rumor scout Apple,

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:36.240
Northwest and Elon Musk's formula.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.670
Well, actually it's a term that
covers the little side trip

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.460
that an employee takes when
they're supposed to be on duty.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.590
And of course, when accidents happen,

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.990
who's responsible when an
employee does something

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.480
that's on their own, but on company time?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.570
Well, generally employers are liable

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.710
for any damages caused by their employees.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.400
But if someone is acting way
outside the scope of their job,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:56.870
the employer might be able to show

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.520
that the employees should
be held legally responsible.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.470
A Frolic is when an
employee totally departs

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:03.800
from his or her duties.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.170
Let's say your boss sends
you to OfficeMax for supplies

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.120
but on the way there
you stop off at a casino

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.520
to play some slot machines for an hour.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:10.353
And on the way back

00:08:10.353 --> 00:08:11.400
you get into a traffic accident.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.530
The trip to the casino
is completely unrelated

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.510
to your job duties.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.700
So the employer won't
be legally responsible.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.533
On the other hand

00:08:17.533 --> 00:08:20.420
a near detour is just
more of a minor deviation

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.500
that probably doesn't absolve
your employer of liability.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.990
For example, if you go to
OfficeMax, in our example

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.030
and then stop at
Starbucks on your way back

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.780
and then get into a traffic accident.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.510
In this instance you've probably only made

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.310
a slight deviation from your job duty

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.490
so the accident may be
within the scope of your job

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.570
and your employer might
have to pay for the damages.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.740
But that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term,

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.130
quiet enjoyment.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.520
Now is quiet enjoyment the nap
time at a Montessori school

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.240
after you have already played
a game of heads up seven up?

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.160
Is it something your
parents desperately want

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.610
especially during COVID

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:55.700
or is it a cover band for quiet riot?

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:57.660
Well, actually quite enjoyment comes up

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.580
in the real estate context.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.610
It's usually a covenant of quiet enjoyment

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.390
and it is one of the promises
that a property owner

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.730
or tenant gets as part of his or her right

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.880
to possess and use the property.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.210
This means that the person has the right

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:11.500
to enjoy the property
without interference.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.290
This covers things like a
landlord repeatedly harassing

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:17.140
a tenant or vermin inside
the walls making noises

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.290
at all hours of the day and night.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:20.770
If you are renting property,

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.810
you have the right to quiet enjoyment

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.880
and that might mean you're
able to listen to quiet right.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.730
But that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:31.750
This one is one of my favorites,
a fertile octogenarian.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.810
Now is a fertile octogenarian

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.630
Mick Jagger, Larry
King, a rejected Pokemon

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:40.780
or the title of TLC's
newest hit reality show,

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.200
I'm marrying a fertile octogenarian?

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.480
Well, a fertile octogenarian
may actually refer

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.200
to all of those things,
but in a legal context

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.040
it means something very specific.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:51.590
This usually involves property law

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:54.470
and it's the source of many
headaches for law students

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:56.890
because a fertile octogenarian
is a fictitious character

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.080
that we make up in property
law to demonstrate the concept

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.710
against the rule against perpetuities.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:02.860
And while it often takes a long time

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:03.693
to wrap your head around it,

00:10:03.693 --> 00:10:05.310
it's actually kind of a simple concept

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:06.570
that is meant to make sure

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.840
that there is certainty
about who gets what.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.490
If you're going to give property

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.190
to a certain category of people

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.900
you have to know that all
of those peoples are certain

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:18.840
within 21 years of the death
of the person who is living.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.160
So for example, if you said,
I give all of my property

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.760
to aunt Bertha who was 100 years old

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.000
and all of aunt Bertha's children.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.320
Well, the thing is,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.880
even though aunt Bertha is 100 years old,

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.780
the law presumes that she
could have more children.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:34.870
So that bequest doesn't work

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.130
because it violates the
rule against perpetuities

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:39.810
because we assume that even
if you're an octogenarian,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.800
the law thinks that you
still could be fertile.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.490
Look, I don't know, the law is weird.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:44.323
What do you want from me?

00:10:44.323 --> 00:10:47.410
But that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term,

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:48.810
an unborn widow.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.980
Now is an unborn widow
an Amish romance novel?

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:56.190
Is it a sad Bon Iver song
which is there any other kind?

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.740
Or is it a Christian horror movie

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:58.920
about a ghost that was never born

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.030
because the couple used
contraceptives instead?

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:04.980
Well, actually an unborn
widow is actually related

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.240
to the last fertile octogenarian.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.170
Yes, property law is the
gift that keeps on giving.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.700
Now let's see how to complicated will

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.210
and you wanted to leave a
house to, let's say, Gary,

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:17.890
for his life and then to
Gary's widow for her life

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.590
and then to Gary's children,
after they pass away.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:22.930
Now the property can't
be given to Gary's widow

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:24.120
until Gary is dead

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.400
which is totally fine
from a legal perspective.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.180
But property law asks us to
assume the most ridiculous

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.340
far out thing imaginable,
like what happens

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.070
if Gary marries a woman
who isn't even born

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.340
at the time that you
made the gift to Gary?

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.370
What if Gary is actually Mick Jagger

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.250
and you left him a house back in 1980

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:43.710
but Mick Jagger later
marries a woman born in 1999.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.320
But here's the problem, he could die

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.960
and then his new bride could outlive him

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.670
for way longer than 21 years.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:49.900
And the rule against perpetuity says

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:52.860
that the interest must
vest within 21 years.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.010
So that transfer would be
invalid from the outset

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.340
because of the possibility
of the unborn widow.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:01.530
But frankly I think my movie
idea is a way better one anyway

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:04.320
but that takes us to the
next ridiculous legal term,

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:05.910
a precocious toddler.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:09.800
Now is a precocious toddler a
hit podcast for moms who know

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.310
that their toddler are gifted?

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:13.570
Is it the name of Gwyneth
Paltrow's baby store?

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:17.300
Is it a never made Adam Sandler
movies starring Kevin James

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.070
as Adam Sandler's son?

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.470
I mean, who wouldn't want to see that?

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:22.320
I'm just full of great movie ideas.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.470
But in reality a precocious
toddler goes back

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:25.850
to property law once more

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.840
and our favorite rule
against perpetuities.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:29.310
Because we've already
talked about how the law

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:31.970
requires you to believe
completely insane things

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:35.100
like an octogenarian, being
fertile or the ability

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:38.020
for anyone to marry someone
who isn't even born yet.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.100
And if you have to assume those things,

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.450
you better believe the
law requires you to think

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:46.800
that everyone one is fertile at birth.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:48.360
I went to law school for this.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.650
Frankly this is all just jiggery-pokery

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.490
which is our next ridiculous legal word.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:57.440
Now is jiggery-pokery a
phrase that will get you fired

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.300
from work because it's racist?

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.340
Is it an old-timey card
game that is racist?

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.160
Is it a very outdated Disney park ride

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.130
that seems just a little bit racist?

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:08.380
Or is it a beloved vintage children's book

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:09.980
that might be racist?

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:12.470
Well actually children
this racist sounding phrase

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:13.370
isn't racist at all.

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:14.923
It's just some bizarre slang from England

00:13:14.923 --> 00:13:16.990
that Justice Antonin Scalia picked up,

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:17.840
but it's a bit of a punch

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.580
because it literally means legal nonsense.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:23.780
The earliest known use of this
phrase was actually in 1845

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:26.100
by the Berkshire Chronicle
in Reading England.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.600
The article was called
protection to agriculture

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:30.690
and it commented that
under the present law

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.080
the averages were made up
so faithfully and fairly

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:35.040
as to prevent any jiggery pokery.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:37.640
And Justice Scalia deployed
the phrase jiggery pokery

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:39.330
to attack Chief Justice Roberts

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.840
for the court six to three decision

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.210
that it was legal for the
IRS to extend tax credits

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:45.280
for the affordable care
act to federal exchanges

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:46.950
as well as those created by the States.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:49.110
So that takes us to our next terms.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.670
Flummery, tommyrot,
balderdash and poppycock.

